# Shared build-context ignore for every `infra/docker/*.Dockerfile`
# (all use `context: .`, the repo root, per `docker-compose.yml`) --
# real, verified gap fixed 2026-08-12: with no `.dockerignore` at all,
# every `docker build` was uploading the *entire* repo as build context
# (every service's `.venv`, `node_modules`, `.git`, notebooks, etc.)
# before a single `COPY` instruction even ran, and `services/ingestion`
# alone was shipping a stale, 10MB+13MB pair of local dev DuckDB files
# (`data/staging/landed.duckdb`, `data/training/master.duckdb` -- real,
# git-tracked bootstrap/dev-seed data, not something a fresh production
# container should start pre-loaded with) baked directly into the image
# via its `COPY services/ingestion .` step, since nothing excluded them.

# Version control / editor / OS cruft
.git
.github
.gitignore
.DS_Store
.claude/

# Python
# `**/` prefix required on every one of these -- real, confirmed-live gap
# (2026-08-19): Docker's `.dockerignore` matching, unlike `.gitignore`,
# does NOT match a bare pattern at arbitrary depth -- `.coverage` alone
# only excludes a file literally at the context root
# (`docker-compose.yml`'s `context: .`), never
# `services/ingestion/.coverage`, which is where every one of these
# actually lives (each service's own `COPY services/<name> .` copies
# from its own subdirectory). Confirmed live: `services/ingestion/
# .coverage`/`.mypy_cache`/`.pytest_cache`/`.ruff_cache` were all still
# landing in the built `ingestion` image despite this section
# "excluding" them by name.
**/__pycache__/
**/*.pyc
**/.mypy_cache/
**/.ruff_cache/
**/.pytest_cache/
**/.coverage
**/*.egg-info/

# Per-service virtualenvs -- each Dockerfile runs its own `uv sync`
# inside its own build stage; a locally-synced `.venv` must never be
# copied in (wrong-platform wheels, stale, and large -- forecast-api's
# alone carries a full local torch install).
**/.venv/

# Node / dashboard
node_modules/
**/node_modules/
services/dashboard/.next/
*.tsbuildinfo

# Env files -- never let a real secret ride into an image layer, even
# as defense in depth beyond "no Dockerfile COPYs .env directly" (none
# do today -- this doesn't fix a live leak, it forecloses a future one).
.env
.env.*
!*.env.example

# Local runtime data artifacts -- real, git-tracked files meant as dev
# bootstrap/seed data, not something a fresh prod container should ship
# with baked in (real data flows through R2/Postgres at runtime).
# Trained ML artifacts under `data/staging/models/**` are NOT matched by
# this (the pattern below only excludes `*.duckdb*` files specifically)
# -- those stay, since the anomaly-detection pipeline actually loads
# them at runtime and they're small (~5MB total).
**/data/staging/*.duckdb*
**/data/training/*.duckdb*

# Jupyter notebooks -- dev/EDA scratchpads, never imported by any
# service's own runtime code.
**/notebooks/
*.ipynb

# Celery Beat's local schedule file / Redis dump -- local runtime state
# from `make dev`, not source. `**/` prefix required -- same real,
# confirmed-live gap this file's own Python section above now documents
# (a bare pattern only matches at the context root, never
# `services/ingestion/celerybeat-schedule*`, where this actually lives).
# Confirmed live: a host-written, macOS-native GNU-dbm-format
# `celerybeat-schedule` file (from a local `make ingestion` run) baked
# straight into the `ingestion-beat` image via this exact gap, and the
# container's own Celery Beat process crash-looped on it every single
# start with `dbm.error: db type could not be determined` -- a stale
# host artifact silently overriding whatever fresh schedule file the
# container would otherwise have started from cleanly.
**/celerybeat-schedule*
**/dump.rdb
